{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790290028,"nonce":"xbzMAUCttpWBSZUxWs7FMgD2gRg=","title":"Conformance cases whose pinned output only holds on POSIX","message":"The Python and Lua conformance suites pin CPython's and Lua 5.4's output as produced on a POSIX host, and the native-tests job runs them on Windows too. Read against Windows, these cases print something else there, whether or not the frontend is right:\n\n- python `scope/rebound_locals.py` opened `/dev/null`, which does not exist on Windows. Rewritten to open the case's own file and read zero bytes; CPython's output is unchanged.\n- python `modules/paths_hashes_format.py`: `os.path.join(\"a\", \"b.txt\")` goes through `std::path::Path::join`, so zypy prints `a\\b.txt` on Windows, as CPython does there; the pin says `a/b.txt`.\n- lua `stdlib/os.lua`: `name:match(\"^/\")` on `os.tmpname()`, `os.getenv(\"HOME\")`, the text `No such file or directory` and its errno, `os.execute(\"true\")` (no `true` under cmd.exe).\n- lua `stdlib/files.lua`: `io.popen(\"echo piped; exit 3\")` and `io.popen(\"cat \u003e ...\", \"w\")` are sh syntax and commands.\n\nNone of this has been observed in a log: the Windows job has never reached these suites (compile errors, the build-script stack overflow, then the bench_memory_probe hang in cd215b0 stop it first). Each needs a decision: make the case portable, pin a Windows expectation beside the POSIX one, or declare the case POSIX-only in the harness.","files":null}]}